One puzzle game which would fit reasonably well into a roguelike
framework is `Inertia', as seen on this page:

  http://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/puzzles/

All you'd need would be a type of floor tile which the player slid
across[1], plus some means of enforcing the collection of a bunch of
things before getting to the exit on the far side. Inertia levels
can be usefully randomly generated, meaning it wouldn't have to be a
question of having a fixed set of levels which people got bored of.

[1] (Encountering the Quest for the first time this week, I was
actually slightly surprised when I _didn't_ slide uncontrollably
across the ice in Valkyrie Home 1.)

You might also be able to do something with Minesweeper if you tried
hard. Have the level littered with trap doors which can't be found
no matter how much you search, disable trap detection, engrave the
number of nearby trap doors on each non-trap-door floor tile, and
provide a means for the player to mark a tile as `known trap'
without stepping on to it. Minesweeper levels can be auto-generated
to ensure solubility from a given starting point (also demonstrated
on the puzzle page given above), so if you shaped the level in such
a way that it enforced you treading on the same first square every
time, it ought to work fine. (You'd have to adjust the generation
algorithm a bit to take account of the fact that the player could
only `click on' (step on to) a floor tile adjacent to one he'd
already mapped, but I think that would be doable.) I think I'd be
inclined not to enforce the solution of the whole grid, either;
simply have the player start at (say) the top left corner and work
their way across to the staircase in the bottom right without
falling down a hole.

At some point I'd like to at least have a _go_ at auto-generating
random Sokoban levels so I can add Sokoban to that puzzle
collection. I have half a plan for this but have yet to put it into
practice. It's unclear whether I'd be able to produce levels of
anything like the same quality and difficulty as human-designed ones
can be, _but_ while that might be a downside for my puzzle
collection it might arguably be a plus point for NetHack: the
average hand-crafted Sokoban level is designed to be a true puzzle,
to baffle people for hours, to be practically impossible to solve on
sight, instead requiring many retries and possibly a client with
undo. That's also fine for NH's Sokoban _if_ they're always the same
levels, but if they were different every time then I think you would
actually want them to be a bit easier because the player would only
ever get one shot at any given level. (Unless it was your intention
that all NH players would routinely cut and paste each Sokoban level
out of NH into an external Sokoban client, try it there repeatedly
until they got it right, and then replay those moves in NetHack.
Doesn't seem right to me. I mean, I did that once for the current
Sokoban branch, but now I just follow the notes I made.)

Finally, one completely silly idea: have a small 4x4 level in which
the player's @ sign acts as the empty square in a 15-puzzle! 16
squares of room, one player arriving on a staircase in the top left,
the other fifteen squares are full of peaceful creatures which you
displace in the same way as pets, by swapping places with the one
you try to move on to. (I know that's not _always_ what happens with
pets, but in this case there'd consistently be no other square for a
creature to move on to!) Only when the creatures are lined up in the
right order does the door you're trying to get through at the bottom
right magically unlock...
